Repro (dev 129f21560a, $JET_RUN_ROOT/Main/enc/main.jet):
struct R { a: String; fn encode(self) -> String { self.a }; fn decode(raw: String) -> R { R{a: raw} } }
jet run reports E0105 '`encode` is defined twice' and '`decode` is defined twice' at the struct name, though each is defined once. The second definition is the synthesized Encode/Decode hook method; sema_checked_member_names (Compiler/JetSema/Source/Sema/Registration/Checked.jet:3153-3190) errs when an ordinary method comes first and a hook with the same name follows. Real code hit: Jetpack ProducerRecord, HangarArchive, CacheBinding, ServiceProcessState (Store/Services) — this blocks every Jetpack bootstrap area check. Acceptance: a user-defined encode/decode method on a struct is either accepted (the derived hook is not synthesized or does not collide) or rejected with a correct, single diagnostic per the language spec; the repro and the Jetpack Trust/CLI area checks pass; UI snapshot if a diagnostic changes.

On dev 73e3477e30 (fixed-point s2), jet run of a single file with an inline module ICEs instead of running or giving a user diagnostic:

    module tokens {
        Token :: distinct Int
        fn make(n: Int) -> Token { Token.from_int(n) }
    }
    fn run() {
        t :: tokens.make(5)
        print(t.raw() + 1)
    }

Output: 'internal compiler error: semantic declaration checking did not return a complete resolved package graph (3 of 3 modules checked) / module:14:source-root:0::run.jet: inline module tokens is not lowered'. Repro: $JET_SCRATCH_ROOT/lanes/Main/q/p4846b/run.jet. A qualified type path from outside (tokens.Token.from_int(7)) separately reports E0107 'Nothing named tokens exists here'.

Done when: the program prints 6 on jet run and --release, or the checker gives a registered diagnostic if inline modules are not part of the language; a UI case or example proves it.

questions.answer:
The code may run again; the changes never apply twice. Under option A, if another task changed a value this block read, the attempt is thrown away before it touches any Shared value, and locals the block changed are restored by the existing auto-snapshot. The block then runs again on fresh values, and only that final attempt commits, exactly once. So repeated work is computation, not modification. print inside the block is held and written once, after the commit. Irreversible effects are already banned inside the block (E0746).

Conflicts are not errors, so there is nothing for the user to handle. I also added a progress rule to A: after a few conflicting attempts, the next attempt locks everything it touched before running, so a busy transaction always finishes.

For real failures, what you describe already exists. A `return Err(...)` or `?` inside #Transact rolls back every change, the caller handles the error, and `tx.on_rollback` runs code only when that happens:

fn transfer(from: Shared<Account>, to: Shared<Account>, amount: Int) -> Int TxnErr! {
    #Transact(tx) {
        tx.on_rollback(() -> print("transfer rolled back"))
        if from.balance < amount -> return Err(TxnErr.Overdraft)
        from.balance -= amount
        to.balance += amount
    }
    Ok(amount)
}

moved :: transfer(a, b, 100) ?? 0   // on failure nothing changed; retry here if you want

A handler attached to the block itself would be a second way to do what `??`, a result table, and on_rollback already do, which the one-mechanism rule (I8) argues against. I have not probed a failing Shared transaction on the current build; card #3967's stress test will cover it. If you still want a block-attached handler, say so and I will ballot it.

